One thing worth being honest about: queue.pop() here is safe only because everything in this example runs inside a single JS process, so there's no risk of two workers grabbing the same item at once. A real work queue, the kind multiple separate worker processes on different machines pull from, has to solve that exact problem, two workers racing to claim the same job, as part of the queue system itself. That's the same coordination problem from Unit 1, just relocated into infrastructure you'd typically rely on rather than code you'd write yourself.

## Exercise

Task: Add a seventh job to the list, then run the batch with 2 workers and with 7 workers, in addition to the 1, 3, and 6 worker counts already in the file.

Starting point: The queue.js and workQueue.js files above.

You'll know it works when: the total time for each worker count roughly matches (number of jobs / number of workers) * 200 ms, rounded up, for every count you test. Add a one-line comment noting at what point adding more workers stops meaningfully helping, and why that point exists.

## Real World

The Notes section's queue is an in-memory array, safe only inside one process. RabbitMQ is a real, widely used message broker that provides the same push/pop shape as a durable, network-reachable service multiple independent worker processes, or containers, can actually compete over.

```yaml
# docker-compose.yml
services:
  rabbitmq:
    image: rabbitmq:3-management
    ports:
      - "5672:5672"
      - "15672:15672" # management UI
```

```js
// producer.js
import amqp from "amqplib";

const connection = await amqp.connect("amqp://localhost");
const channel = await connection.createChannel();
const QUEUE = "resize-jobs";
await channel.assertQueue(QUEUE, { durable: true });

for (let id = 1; id <= 6; id++) {
  const job = { id, task: "resize image" };
  channel.sendToQueue(QUEUE, Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(job)), { persistent: true });
  console.log(`sent job ${id}`);
}

setTimeout(() => connection.close(), 500);
```

```js
// worker.js
import amqp from "amqplib";

const workerId = process.argv[2] || "0";
const QUEUE = "resize-jobs";

const connection = await amqp.connect("amqp://localhost");
const channel = await connection.createChannel();
await channel.assertQueue(QUEUE, { durable: true });
channel.prefetch(1); // only ever hand this worker one unacknowledged job at a time

console.log(`worker ${workerId} waiting for jobs`);

channel.consume(QUEUE, async (msg) => {
  if (msg === null) return;
  const job = JSON.parse(msg.content.toString());
  console.log(`worker ${workerId} processing job ${job.id}`);
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 200));
  console.log(`worker ${workerId} finished job ${job.id}`);
  channel.ack(msg); // tell RabbitMQ this job is truly done, only now does it leave the queue for good
});
```

